{"parentUuid": "0b7a6557-3590-4283-a1e4-1f873286f48a", "isSidechain": false, "type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": "is the unique index going to lock the table on a big debits table?"}, "uuid": "9ef14c0f-92c4-42b1-8dd9-6c8d0b544f76", "timestamp": "2026-09-26T20:09:56.144Z", "cwd": "/home/dev/billing", "sessionId": "6b854898-b35a-45ed-879c-4b315b5f9c99", "gitBranch": "main"}
{"parentUuid": "71cc7016-aa46-4628-9a8e-4541c7a7cb14", "isSidechain": false, "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001", "id": "msg_011CfSf6wfPRi5JzoVukcrb8", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Yes, that's a critical concern. Creating a unique index on a large table can cause significant locking issues:\n\n**Default behavior (blocking):**\n- Most SQL databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL) acquire an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock during index creation\n- This blocks all reads and writes until the index is done\n- On a large debits table, this could mean minutes of downtime\n\n**Solutions:**\n- **PostgreSQL**: Use `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` — builds the index without holding an exclusive lock, but takes longer and uses more resources\n- **MySQL**: Use `ALGORITHM=INPLACE, LOCK=NONE` (8.0+) — similar no"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 10, "output_tokens": 645, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24245, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 661}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "type": "assistant", "uuid": "1b0ebc3b-8671-4452-8c53-8de661d0c4f3", "timestamp": "2026-09-26T20:10:03.931Z", "cwd": "/home/dev/billing", "sessionId": "6b854898-b35a-45ed-879c-4b315b5f9c99", "gitBranch": "main"}
